[{:seon.task/name "the-branch-roster-is-compacted"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: the branch roster is a konserve append log (`datahike.roster`), one entry a `branch!` or delete, never compacted. ``d/branches`` reads the whole log, one konserve read an entry (11.7k ticks and 33 KB each, measured 2026-11-11), deleted branches included: 94k ticks at 1 branch, 1.25 M at 100, 11.8 M and 34 MB at 1,000. A root's store gains a branch an agent, so the log only grows. Correct: the log is folded into one value when it is read past a bound, or a delete rewrites it, so ``d/branches`` costs the live branches (one read when compact). Upstream's own versioning first (`branch-history`, durable GC roots): take its shape if it has one. Proof when built: ``d/branches`` after n branches made and deleted costs what the live ones cost. Absorbed in curation 2026-11-14 (same subject and evidence): seon.db/a-long-running-store-stays-bounded (A user's store runs for months with no one erasing it)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{datahike.versioning-test/the-branch-roster-is-compacted}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-merge-takes-a-branchs-facts-unchanged"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: deps (root's order 2026-11-16: deps leads; data consults and owns seon.db/an-id-is-made-where-its-entity-is-made, an input). The owner: a branch's work merges into main as it is, nothing rewritten (the same eids, each branch transaction's identity kept); merges make sense and cause no conflicts, a needed constraint found and stated; RESEARCH FIRST, the best answer not a fast one. His test case: a message written on main, seen in a branch by a merge down, then the branch merged up: one message in one consistent history, or a duplicate accepted knowingly. STATUS: research done 2026-11-16 (deps); the answer and its plan below, not built. FINDINGS [M unless marked]: (1) Seon already carries a branch's datoms with their original tx ids into main (``seon.merge/carried-tx``, merge.clj:503-532; merge-main adds head's datoms and their tx entities, :665-670; Datahike's carried-tx check, db/transaction.cljc:908-911, needs every carried tx at or below this transaction's own id). (2) So main's tx order is already not its commit order: of main's last 2,000 commits on the standing store, 219 hold a tx above a newer commit's lowest tx, 2,826 datoms [root result/bb84ef2c]; and none of the 2,000 is a two-parent commit: a merge into main is recorded single-parent, so the commit graph does not know a branch was merged. (3) What reads tx numbers as apply order, and is wrong after any merge today (survey, file:line in deps' notes of 2026-11-16 below): as-of/since (db.cljc:144-153), the history fold (db.cljc:195-212; seon problems.clj:304, merge.clj:523, union.clj:65), the inbox's since (server.clj:468, system.clj:2164,2183: a message merged below a since already returned is never delivered), commit-tx-range/point-base/walk-to-tx/commit-holding-tx (versioning.cljc:673-729,790-824), fork-database's max-tx walk (:1080-1090), seon ctx/wire.clj:83-88. Clean of tx numbers: merge-base (by generation), d/changes (by :base, fixed in Datahike deda581a), force-branch!. (4) Id partition (datahike/constants.cljc:5-14: e < tx0=2^29 <= tx <= txmax=2^40-1 < ce0=2^40 <= content <= emax=2^60-1) and its readers: max-eid kept below tx0 (db.cljc:1068-1076, db/transaction.cljc:110,561,682), next-eid's exhaustion at tx0 (:89), content-eid's range (:67-68), entity-id's check (:1087), 'a number >= tx0 is a tx' (api/impl.cljc:180, query.cljc:2763), every slice's sentinels (40+ sites, need only the constants), whole? in index/persistent_set.cljc:319-321 (every id within emax), the planner using max-eid as the population (query.cljc:3716, query/estimate.cljc:319-320, query/plan.cljc:1234-1236: time ids would make it the clock). (5) Width: leaves store Datom fields as zigzag varints (konserve jfasl.ss:12,83-92): a tx near 2^59 costs 9 bytes against 5 today, +4 B a datom; ids change every leaf's bytes and address, so the change is an erase and re-import, the owner's act. (6) The constraint that makes merges conflict-free already exists as a rule, not a rail: docs/sharing.md#where-a-fact-lives, each attribute of an entity is set on one branch; messages and root's observations live on main only and no merge carries them. (7) A merge up does not move the merge base [M]: on the standing store, merges of main INTO a branch are two-parent commits (last 300 commits: :main/tests 103, :main/task-session-data 33, web 8; root result/c5c6f4a6), but a merge INTO main is a single-parent force-branch!, so main's graph never names a merged branch; on a scratch store at deps' agent, after a single-parent merge up the branch's merge base stays before its own commit (generation 2 against its head's 3), so its merged change counts as its own again at its next merge; with the branch named as a second parent (force-branch!'s parents argument, which Datahike already takes) the base is the branch's head [deps' agent result/c6d7f72d, result/b357583a]. This is the fragility in the owner's message case, and its fix needs nothing new in Datahike. (8) d/changes' records were wrong for plain d/transact connections (each commit named the writer's first base and held every datom since); fixed on main c289a21cb0 (Datahike 42f2523d). (9) A merge commit keeps one changes record, against the branch head it replaced (force-branch! :changes), so d/changes from the merge base (main's head at the merge) to the branch head never meets it on the :base walk (data, 2026-11-16: seon.merge/decide asks exactly that after a branch's first merge; probe in task seon.merge/a-merge-replays-the-sources-net-change). Since c289a21cb0 that walk answers :changes-not-ancestor at once (it ran to the store's first commit, :changes-unrecorded). Candidate, data's: a merge commit keeps its change against each of its parents, so the walk follows whichever parent leads to from, O(d); to weigh with (7)'s two-parent merge up, which records the same parents. (10) The owner's case on a scratch Datahike [deps' agent result/d1027635]: a message written on main after a branch was cut, carried down into the branch with its own tx, the branch's own fact carried up with its own tx, and the message carried up again: main's history holds the message once ([3 :msg \"hello\" tm true], one datom) and both branches hold the same two facts in the same tx order. A carry is a set union of datoms at their own [e a v tx], so it is idempotent: one message, one order, by construction. What fails is knowledge order: the branch's fact lands on main at tx 536870914, below the 536870915 an inbox on main had already read past, so a tx-number since never returns it (the inbox's own bug, server.clj inbox-messages and system.clj await-message filter (> (:tx %) since)). (11) d/changes is knowledge order, and is missing one piece [result/baa299d7]: after a single-parent merge up, d/changes from main's head before the merge to its head after names the branch's fact and the carried tx entities; after a two-parent merge up (merge base right, (7)) it answers [], because the merge commit keeps one changes record, against the branch head ((9)). Both halves are needed together: two parents for the merge base, a changes record against each parent for d/changes. (12) The field [helper survey, primary docs]: Datomic has one axis t from one transactor and no merge; XTDB has two axes (valid time, settable; system time, one log) and no branches; Dolt's one axis is the commit DAG (AS OF by commit), keys unchanged across merges only when they are UUIDs or the one server-wide counter (its blog: clones' auto-increment keys conflict), merge cell-wise three-way, a merge commit two parents; git's merge commit makes the merged tip an ancestor so the next base is that tip; Automerge and Yjs keep op ids (counter, actor) and merge as a union of ops, a concurrent write to one register kept as several values with a deterministic winner; HLC (Kulkarni et al. 2014): l := max(l, l.m, pt) with a counter, e hb f implies (l.e,c.e) < (l.f,c.f); Snowflake 41 ms + 10 worker + 12 sequence bits, collision-free only with unique worker numbers. None has both a per-fact time and per-branch knowledge; Seon already has both: a tx (made once, carried unchanged) and a commit (a whole db value, what that branch knew). (13) Costs on the standing store [root result/ae411e6b, result/d8f9e714]: main holds 648,993 current datoms, 1,367,009 history datoms and 29,539 transactions; max-tx is tx0+57,375 (the store's counter across all branches), max-eid 37,140. d/changes on main: 1 commit 12 datoms 25k ticks; 10 commits 137 datoms 150k ticks; 100 commits 1,198 datoms 7.0M ticks (two runs alike): ~1.1k ticks a datom at 10 commits but ~5.8k at 100, so its cost grows faster than the datoms it answers (hypothesis: net-changes or the walk; settle by ticks of each at 100 and 1,000 commits). An id at 2^59 is a 9-byte zigzag varint against 5 bytes for a tx near 2^29 and 3 for an eid near 37k (konserve jfasl.ss:12,83-92): time ids cost +4 B a datom's tx and +6 B a counter-minted e or ref, in every index copy, and every leaf is rewritten (an erase and re-import). ANSWER. Fact time and knowledge time are two dimensions, and Datahike already keeps both: a datom's tx is when and where the fact was made, carried unchanged by every merge and shared by every branch (as-of by tx keeps meaning 'made at or before', db.cljc:144-153 compares <= only, so tx ids need be increasing, never dense); a commit is what one branch knew, its db a whole value, and 'what is new to me since I last read' is d/changes from the commit I read to the head, O(d). The owner's case then holds by construction: one message (union at [e a v tx]), one order (tx), and every reader that asks 'new since' sees a merged fact exactly once. Three changes do it, none erases the store: (A) a merge up records the merged branch as a second parent (force-branch!'s parents, which Datahike takes), so the merge base advances (7); (B) a merge commit keeps a changes record against each parent (data's candidate in (9)), so d/changes walks to from in O(d) whichever side from is on; (C) every reader of 'new since' moves from a tx number to a commit: the inbox and await-message (a since becomes the commit the reader last read, d/changes filtered to :seon.message/to), ctx/wire.clj:63,87,101, render/ui.clj:841,893,1713, seon.db/at's {:t} (db.clj:1297-1313), system.clj:2234's commit-at-tx; readers of fact time keep tx (``seon.merge/overtaken``, merge.clj:414, which orders by :db/txInstant today and would read tx order directly; problems.clj:347; reconcile.clj:986). Datahike's commit-tx-range, point-base, walk-to-tx, commit-holding-tx and fork-database's int :at (versioning.cljc:673-729,790-824,1080-1090) assume one store-wide counter in commit order, already false after a carried merge: they are deleted with their one Seon caller (system.clj:2234), commits named by id instead. The one conflict a union can meet is a cardinality-one or unique attribute of one entity given two values on two branches; its constraint stands (docs/sharing.md#where-a-fact-lives: each attribute of an entity is set on one branch), with identities as content ids (seon.db/an-id-is-made-where-its-entity-is-made, part 1) so both sides name one entity, and a breach is an unmerged entry the agent resolves (THE FOCUS), never a silent last-writer win. IDS: minting where made (time ids, an HLC tx) is not needed for this case inside one store: the counter already gives ids unchanged by a merge, and a write after a merge down already takes a higher tx than all it merged (causal order). What it buys is no shared counter page (native/ids.c, the boot scan) and stores on several machines; it costs the erase and the bytes in (13). For data's questions: as-of needs ids only increasing; entity ids above the tx range need every 'a number >= tx0 is a tx' reader ((4): api/impl.cljc:180, query.cljc:2763, max-eid and next-eid, the planner's population) turned into a tag test; that is deps' Datahike work when the owner gives the erase. PLAN (A, B, C; the id change apart, on the owner's erase). PRIOR WORK: findings (1)-(13); ``seon.merge/carried-tx``, force-branch!'s parents, d/changes's :base walk, the inbox's since; nothing beside them. COST: a merge up adds one parent id and one changes record (~12 datoms a commit on main, measured) to the merge commit, O(d); an inbox read becomes d/changes over the commits since the reader's last (1 commit 25k ticks, 10 commits 150k) filtered to its own links, against today's O(messages to it) seek (~10k ticks a message): the same order for a reader that reads often, and correct; (13)'s superlinear d/changes is fixed first if a reader reads across 100 commits. MEASURED: as (10), (11), (13). Order: B (Datahike, deps), then A (seon.writer's merge into main, data), then C (inbox and await-message, flow; ctx and render readers, context and web), each red first, the proof below first."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.merge-test/a-message-merged-down-then-up-is-one-message-in-one-order}
  :seon.task/estimate #:seon.merge{merge-main {:n 2000, :order :linear, :ticks 90000000, :bytes 300000000}}
  :seon.task/needs #{}}
]
